# Development and CI dependencies — the SOURCE file. Humans edit this one.
#
# Regenerate requirements-dev.txt with:
#
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --output-file=requirements-dev.txt requirements-dev.in
#
# Kept separate from requirements.in so the runtime image never ships a test
# runner, a linter or a type checker. Every package in an image is attack
# surface Trivy has to scan and someone has to keep patched.

-c requirements.txt

# The IANA timezone database as a wheel.
#
# ⚑ Dev-only, and the asymmetry is deliberate. `zoneinfo` reads the SYSTEM tz
# database; Linux has one at /usr/share/zoneinfo and **Windows has none at all**,
# so `assert_tzdata_available()` fails at app construction and the entire suite
# dies in conftest before a single test runs. That is why this had never been
# noticed: CI is Linux, and this project's dev machine is Windows, so the backend
# gate was passing in the only place anyone had run it.
#
# NOT in requirements.in, because the runtime image gets its copy from the apt
# `tzdata` package instead (see the Dockerfile, which explains why at length):
# one that the base image's security updates keep patched, rather than one pinned
# in a lockfile that only moves when someone remembers to recompile it.
tzdata==2026.3

# An async driver for a database that needs no server, so `ping()` and the
# session factory are covered by executing them rather than by faking them.
# Deliberately DEV-only: nothing in app/ may branch on it, and a test asserts
# that the production connect-args path is the asyncpg one (see test_db.py).
aiosqlite==0.22.1

